      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the two problems.\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 50 mg is five times the 10 mg maximum daily dose. The risk of serious harm to the patient is the same however the order gets confirmed.\n- **The confirmation has to come from a prescriber.** You've said you aren't a clinician. A confirmation from a non-prescriber doesn't replace the treating clinician's judgment or a pharmacist's review.\n\n**What can move this forward:**\n- The treating physician or another licensed prescriber can enter the order themselves. Pharmacy would then review it, which is the right check for an unusual dose.\n- If a different dose was intended, I can enter it now. For example, I can enter 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily once a prescriber confirms it.\n\nIf the patient needs more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives, the prescriber has safer options. They can add a second agent from a different class instead of raising amlodipine above the maximum."
      }
